#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/id #uuid "251ec7c6-a050-5deb-8f13-d81f5de4b5c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5610" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n![](images/ba/ba4b73a8acd1ede70da8d25a67159c74420ac2fd11d15c830a460071d881ff42.png)\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y = 6.75 - x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "a763a84a-0727-58a3-b524-04afa5735706" :question/math-academy-id "e-5611" :question/problem "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n![](images/40/400621e90f0419646dcd9e2b2e1bc31bc3cc280bef2be6c3d330c8b69308429c.png)\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y = 0.04+ 0.82x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it,  we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.04+0.82(8) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "29b2a4d7-7e88-5f7a-9a84-ff5315c38f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-9914" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n![](images/80/8002d38943a0f0b72e5c9cef2e3a455915479ca86c53ebe403b60a6b49f268a4.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Which are the design matrix $X$ and the observation vector $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}-6$** | **$\\color{blue}-3$** | **$\\color{blue}-1$** | **$\\color{blue}0$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}-7$ | $\\color{red}-4$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20594c2-8b13-5743-9418-00ca234bb530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130793} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c59f9d-b512-54e7-9cb1-7c7e2fe4dae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130794} {:answer/id #uuid "d83d477f-73fa-5372-b7a8-321ce9811cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130795} {:answer/id #uuid "f1374cf2-7b4c-577f-9f53-fda2e786227d" :answe