\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f72511a9-1a42-54ef-9b41-df77797da95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400793} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1580ff-808f-5338-bc02-463787d3ed1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400794} {:answer/id #uuid "8385f538-3baf-529f-a1c9-a9aec0c55a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400795} {:answer/id #uuid "9e066afe-6d94-535c-96d3-9bb7372cb470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -6 & -4 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400796} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e5182b-8e5c-5824-b79f-61f4f55d243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e5182b-8e5c-5824-b79f-61f4f55d243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400797} :answer-field/id #uuid "98811076-3a99-55d7-b6f9-cbe56c0b8dfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdfe0245-88a8-53ab-a5e5-1583ad02add8" :question/math-academy-id "q-139606" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-8$** | **$-6$** | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-8$** | **$-6$** | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13253f23-aec5-55ad-b4c7-17df66b79ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2+1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269437} {:answer/id #uuid "64399bb9-63ee-5478-9c09-041f12659f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2-1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270160} {:answer/id #uuid "84dd91b7-54c3-5219-9384-e547a61ebd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.4+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270416} {:answer/id #uuid "dd253302-e503-5a9f-b155-23a84fb59209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.4-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271209} {:answer/id #uuid "e0588728-a6d8-5925-a9d4-e429f8b21a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0588728-a6d8-5925-a9d4-e429f8b21a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271239} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f24f5e-041b-5ab7-a484-f879d53d40a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c98cf9f6-90e7-55f2-84a5-a676fae1738c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139617" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=2+1.4x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2edf9f-25b1-5fd5-948c-237e6c5948da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.7-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270211} {:answer/id #uuid "897239e6-c3c2-5611-8c33-8bafe39cfda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6-1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270454} {:answer/id #uuid "b26d58d6-c973-5c1f-91c9-981d64dedc48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6+1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270863} {:answer/id #uuid "bd862383-7cfa-5c21-a439-ce2690a7f88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1.7+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270940} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d0e162-e5b3-5659-ba89-d415deaf13c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1.7-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897239e6-c3c2-5611-8c33-8bafe39cfda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6-1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270454} :answer-field/id #uuid "860792a7-ff74-5496-9949-a0199174c0c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72cc6e31-5631-5692-b09e-242241827364" :question/math-academy-id "q-139618" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1.7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6-1.7x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33827e6-fbe3-5ac0-81bb-afc0b60f6ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369764} {:answer/id #uuid "69f66b87-8a13-5240-a11e-f3b29f44f14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369765} {:answer/id #uuid "15d294b4-85e9-5347-9018-98bd1409800c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369766} {:answer/id #uuid "b5774131-ea9b-5ca6-bb51-7a928a8d2bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369767} {:answer/id #uuid "f267db8f-0f38-543e-b961-caf64449125d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f66b87-8a13-5240-a11e-f3b29f44f14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369765} :answer-field/id #uuid "8078f01b-e510-57a4-8b30-017102a3b605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3afd6077-78d7-5f4e-b464-aa77715a7491" :question/math-academy-id "q-139642" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=4+3x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cb7704-5d65-5a8a-b6f4-5c1f2eca8d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550379} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550476} {:answer/id #uuid "767ed063-1f3b-5581-a169-3766dae98d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550666} {:answer/id #uuid "90a442b4-496e-5aff-8488-442c3527f982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550735} {:answer/id #uuid "ee44493c-7944-5d36-ba66-2bc4ba331705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550476} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139644" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=-3-6x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550495} {:answer/id #uuid "580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550583} {:answer/id #uuid "6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550646} {:answer/id #uuid "b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550855} {:answer/id #uuid "e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550646} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139645" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=4-2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979c5af4-9964-538b-b144-532063ca3f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130823} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d790f1-d20b-5c8a-bbc1-23c707806914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130824} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf336cd-ac26-5998-9be3-a638605f8e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130825} {:answer/id #uuid "64aa2687-8d50-568b-9155-b17f093ad1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130826} {:answer/id #uuid "7d17029a-45a5-5838-89f1-19c40f3f3fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130827}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bb67d2ca-15d7-5506-b1d1-9f75b40c28f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c12a9093-8b33-506e-8833-5f1ad6a9261f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400834} {:answer/id #uuid "032b222d-40e4-532e-af62-dfdc2dcc37e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400835} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce327d5-8771-5e11-b6ab-ff3533542dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400836} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d9746c-39bb-5726-8a32-8963780d412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400837} {:answer/id #uuid "a99677dd-5b51-522d-a3d5-8b761133f62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b222d-40e4-532e-af62-dfdc2dcc37e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400835} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc15713a-b900-5d20-8415-c6b1dd1f9e84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f426df0-1f38-5a2e-b701-5e427a0357ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-139750" :question/problem "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.25$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550380} {:answer/id #uuid "33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.75$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550493} {:answer/id #uuid "bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550887} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550935} {:answer/id #uuid "f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.75$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550887} :answer-field/id #uuid "5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41401" :question/problem "The height (in feet) and the average number of points scored per game (APG) of four players on a basketball team are shown in the following table. \n\n| Height $(x)$ | **$6$** | **$6.5$** | **$6.5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| APG $(y)$ | $5$ | $8$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}?$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24.25 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-24.25+5x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft},$  we substitute $x=6.45$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-24.25+5(6.45) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model,  the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}$ will be $8$  points per game."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$44.7$ inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550376} {:answer/id #uuid "2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.8$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550482} {:answer/id #uuid "54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$46.9$ inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550568} {:answer/id #uuid "d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.1$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550953} {:answer/id #uuid "e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$46.2$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.1$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550953} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/math-academy-id "q-41739" :question/problem "A physics experiment consists of a small object moving along a straight line. The following table contains data collected on the distance traveled by the object (in inches) during the first five seconds. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Distance $(y)$ | $8.6$ | $13.1$ | $24.6$ | $28.1$ | $38.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what distance will the object cover after $6$ seconds, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e9ac6ff-6dc0-50d1-bcf0-e5924b445f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,050$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402738} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84303a-bedd-5991-9237-200188eab34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$950$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402739} {:answer/id #uuid "dc47e4af-c36a-5241-a8ef-baaa45ef2c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$875$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402740} {:answer/id #uuid "21e67319-25a2-5a84-a939-d04c90f738c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402741} {:answer/id #uuid "82ab20d8-e65e-5a0a-ba4c-f261ab35ac4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$850$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e67319-25a2-5a84-a939-d04c90f738c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402741} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3cb994-1bad-5429-8732-d77a5dada295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0007be5-4b39-5b13-9d0c-f56e380ce0f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-41741" :question/problem "A small business collected data on the number of hours of telephone usage and the total amount (in hundreds of dollars) of the company's weekly operating expenses. This data is shown below.   \n\n| Hours $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.4$** | **$2.8$** | **$3.2$** | **$3.6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Expenses $(y)$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours per week, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.4 \\\\ 1 & 2.8 \\\\ 1 & 3.2 \\\\ 1 & 3.6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-1+2.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the amount of the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours, we substitute $x=4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2.5(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the company's weekly operating expenses will be $$900.$ \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=4$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba73043-ba7f-5144-85cc-553d87d248c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$24\\,600$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402745} {:answer/id #uuid "1584ef53-01f2-5061-8e58-39b6ee6c83df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$37\\,300$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402746} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfaef46-165b-54fb-98ec-40334338b6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$20\\,400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402747} {:answer/id #uuid "01465c5f-aea0-556f-b1a7-2a55b583c3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$34\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402748} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5f7e29-cc12-59e3-bba6-2cee48d6debc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$39\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cfaef46-165b-54fb-98ec-40334338b6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$20\\,400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402747} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4ac2e5-74a0-528f-8078-d6aa415b8f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e98728e8-7708-5748-ae2b-39ed7242bc51" :question/math-academy-id "q-41751" :question/problem "A store collected data about its profits (in thousands of dollars) for the year's first five months. This data is shown below.\n\n| Month $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $12$ | $15$ | $16$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the profit in the $6$th month, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10.8 \\\\ 1.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=10.8+1.6x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10.8+1.6(6) \\\\  & =20.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$20\\,400.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}]}
